X I'm going to go around the class and give you each a raisin. Now, what I would
X+ like you to do is to see it as an object  ust imagining that you have never  
X+ seen anything like it before, as if you had dropped in from another planet   
X+ this moment and you had never seen anything like it before in your life.     
X+ Note. There is at least a 10-second pause between phrases, and the           
X+ instructions are delivered in a matter-of-fact way, at a slow but deliberate 
X+ pace, asking the class to do the following: Taking the object and holding it 
X+ in the palm of your hand, or between your finger and thumb. (Pause)Paying    
X+ attention to seeing it. (Pause) Looking at it carefully, as if you had never 
X+ seen such a thing before. (Pause) Turning it over between your fingers.      
X+ (Pause) Exploring its texture between your fingers. (Pause) Examining the    
X+ highlights where the light shines . . . the darker hollows and folds. (Pause)
X+ Letting your eyes explore every part of it, as if you had never seen such a  
X+ thing before. (Pause) And if, while you are doing this, any thoughts come to 
X+ mind about "what a strange thing we are doing" or "what is the point of this"
X+ or "I don't like this," then just noting them as thoughts and bringing your  
X+ awareness back to the object. (Pause) And now, taking the object and holding 
X+ it beneath your nose, and with each in-breath, carefully seeing what you     
X+ notice-does it have a smell? (Pause) And now looking at it again. (Pause) And
X+ now slowly taking the object to your mouth, maybe noticing how your hand and 
X+ arm know exactly where to put it, perhaps noticing what happens in your mouth
X+ as it comes up. (Pause) And then gently placing the object in the mouth,     
X+ noticing how it is "received," without biting it, just exploring the         
X+ sensations of having it in your mouth. (Pause) Exploring the object with your
X+ tongue, noticing the sensations as you move it around the mouth. (Pause)And  
X+ when you are ready, very consciously taking a bite into it and noticing what 
X+ happens-the tastes that it releases. (Pause) Slowly chewing it, . . .        
X+ noticing what's happening in the mouth, . . . the change in consistency of   
X+ the object. (Pause) Then, when you feel ready to swallow, seeing if you can  
X+ first detect the intention to swallow as it comes up, so that even this is   
X+ experienced consciously, before you actually swallow it. (Pause) Finally,    
X+ seeing if you can follow the sensations of swallowing it, sensing it moving  
X+ down to your stomach, any aftertaste, then noticing the absence of the raisin
X+ in the mouth-and what the tongue does when it is gone.                       
E meditation_23;general;prompt;en                                               
M Body Scan Meditation                                                          
U Health                                                                        
Q 20250167_Arbeit.odp                                                           
X 1. Lie down, making yourself comfortable, lying on your back on a mat or rug  
X+ on the floor or on your bed, in a place where you will be warm and           
X+ undisturbed. Allow your eyes to close gently.                                
X 2. Take a few moments to get in touch with the movement of your breath and the
X+ sensations in the body. When you are ready, bring your awareness to the      
X+ physical sensations in your body, especially to the sensations of touch or   
X+ pressure, where your body makes contact with the floor or bed. On each       
X+ outbreath, allow yourself to let go, to sink a little deeper into the mat or 
X+ bed.                                                                         
X 3. Remind yourself of the intention of this practice. Its aim is not to feel  
X+ any different, relaxed, or calm; this may happen or it may not. Instead, the 
X+ intention of the practice is, as best you can, to bring awareness to any     
X+ sensations you detect, as you focus your attention on each part of the body  
X+ in turn.                                                                     
X 4. Now bring your awareness to the physical sensations in the lower abdomen,  
X+ becoming aware of the changing patterns of sensations in the abdominal wall  
X+ as you breathe in, and as you breathe out. Take a few minutes to feel the    
X+ sensations as you breathe in and as you breathe out.                         
X 5. Having connected with the sensations in the abdomen, bring the focus or    
X+ "spotlight" of your awareness down the left leg, into the left foot, and out 
X+ to the toes of the left foot. Focus on each of the toes of the left foot in  
X+ turn, bringing a gentle curiosity to investigate the quality of the          
X+ sensations you find, perhaps noticing the sense of con- tact between the     
X+ toes, a sense of tingling, warmth, or no particular sensation.               
X 6. When you are ready, on an inbreath, feel or imagine the breath entering the
X+ lungs, and then passing down into the abdomen, into the left leg, the left   
X+ foot, and out to the toes of the left foot. Then, on the outbreath, feel or  
X+ imagine the breath coming all the way back up, out of the foot, into the leg,
X+ up through the abdomen, chest, and out through the nose. As best you can,    
X+ continue this for a few breaths, breathing down into the toes, and back out  
X+ from the toes. It may be difficult to get the hang of this-just practice this
X+ "breath- ing into" as best you can, approaching it playfully. 7.Now, when you
X+ are ready, on an outbreath, let go of awareness of the toes, and bring your  
X+ awareness to the sensations on the bottom of your left foot-bringing a       
X+ gentle, investigative awareness to the sole of the foot, the instep, the heel
X+ (e.g., noticing the sensations where the heel makes contact with the mat or  
X+ bed). Experiment with "breathing with" the sensations-being aware of the     
X+ breath in the background, as, in the foreground, you explore the sensations  
X+ of the lower foot.                                                           
X 8. Now allow the awareness to expand into the rest of the foot-to the ankle,  
X+ the top of the foot, and right into the bones and joints. Then, taking a     
X+ slightly deeper breath, directing it down into the whole of the left foot,   
X+ and, as thebreath lets go on the outbreath, let go of the left foot          
X+ completely, allowing the focus of awareness to move into the lower left      
X+ leg-the calf, shin, knee, and so on, in turn.                                
X 9. Continue to bring awareness, and a gentle curiosity, to thephysical        
X+ sensations in each part of the rest of the body in turn-to the upper left    
X+ leg, the right toes, right foot, right leg, pelvic area, back, abdo- men,    
X+ chest, fingers, hands, arms, shoulders, neck, head, and face. In each area,  
X+ as best you can, bring the same detailed level of awareness and gentle       
X+ curiosity to the body sensations present. As you leave each major area,      
X+ "breathe in" to it on the inbreath, and let go of that region on the         
X+ outbreath.                                                                   
X 10. When you become aware of tension, or of other intense sensations in a     
X+ particular part of the body, you can "breathe in" to them-using the inbreath 
X+ gently to bring awareness right into the sensations, and, as best you can,   
X+ have a sense of their letting go, or releasing, on the outbreath.            
X 11. The mind will inevitably wander away from the breath and the body from    
X+ time to time. That is entirely normal. It is what minds do. When you notice  
X+ it, gently acknowledge it, noticing where the mind has gone off to, and then 
X+ gently return your attention to the part of the body you intended to focus   
X+ on.                                                                          
X 12. After you have "scanned" the whole body in this way, spend a few minutes  
X+ being aware of a sense of the body as a whole, and of the breath flowing     
X+ freely in and out of the body.                                               
X 13. If you find yourself falling asleep, you might find it helpful to prop    
X+ your head up with a pillow, open your eyes, or do the practice sitting up    
X+ rather than lying down.                                                      
E meditation_24;general;prompt;en                                               
M Tips for Body Scan                                                            
U Health                                                                        
Q 20250169_Arbeit.odp                                                           
X 1. Regardless of what happens (e.g., if you fall asleep, lose concentration,  
X+ keep thinking of other things or focusing on the wrong bit of body, or not   
X+ feeling anything), persist with it! These are your experiences in the moment.
X+ See if it is possible to be aware of them all, just as they are.             
X 2. If your mind is wandering a lot, simply note the thoughts (as passing      
X+ events), then bring the mind gently back to the body scan.                   
X 3. Let go of ideas of "success," "failure," "doing it really well," or "trying
X+ to purify the body." This is not a competition. It is not a skill for which  
X+ you need to strive. The only discipline involved is regular and frequent     
X+ practice. Just do it with an attitude of openness and curiosity, then allow  
X+ the rest to take care of itself.                                             
X 4. Let go of any expectations about what the body scan will do for you:       
X+ Imagine it as a seed you have planted. The more you poke around and          
X+ interfere, the less it will be able to develop. So with the body scan, just  
X+ give it the right conditions-peace and quiet, regular and frequent practice. 
X+ That is all. The more you try to influence what it will do for you, the less 
X+ it will do.                                                                  
X 5. Try approaching your experience in each moment with the attitude: "OK,     
X+ that's just the way things are right now." If you try to fight off unpleasant
X+ thoughts, feelings, or body sensations, the upsetting feelings will only     
X+ distract you from doing anything else. Be aware, be nonstriving, be in the   
X+ moment, accept things as they are.                                           
E meditation_25;general;prompt;en                                               
M Tips for Body Scan                                                            
U Health                                                                        
Q 20250170_Arbeit.odp                                                           
X 1. Settle into a comfortable sitting position, either on a straight-backed    
X+ chair or a soft surface on the floor, with your buttocks supported by        
X+ cushions or a low stool. If you use a chair, it is very helpful to sit away  
X+ from the back of the chair, so that your spine is self-supporting. If you sit
X+ on the floor, it is helpful if your knees actu- ally touch the floor;        
X+ experiment with the height of the cushions or stool until you feel           
X+ comfortably and firmly supported. Whatever you sit on, arrange things so that
X+ your knees are lower than your hips.                                         
X 2. Allow your back to adopt an erect, dignified, and comfortable posture. If  
X+ sitting on a chair, place your feet flat on the floor, with your legs        
X+ uncrossed. Gently close your eyes.                                           
X 3. Bring your awareness to the level of physical sensations by focusing your  
X+ atten- tion on the sensations of touch and pressure in your body where it    
X+ makes contact with the floor and whatever you are sitting on. Spend a minute 
X+ or two exploring these sensations, just as in the body scan.                 
X 4. Now bring your awareness to the changing patterns of physical sensations in
X+ the lower abdomen as the breath moves in and out of your body. (When you     
X+ first try this practice, it may be helpful to place your hand on your lower  
X+ abdomen and become aware of the changing pattern of sensations where your    
X+ hand makes contact with your abdomen. Having "tuned in" to the physical      
X+ sensations in this area in this way, you can remove your hand and continue to
X+ focus on the sensa- tions in the abdominal wall.)                            
X 5. Focus your awareness on the sensations of slight stretching as the         
X+ abdominal wall rises with each inbreath, and of gentle deflation as it falls 
X+ with each outbreath. As best you can, follow with your awareness the changing
X+ physical sensations in the lower abdomen all the way through as the breath   
X+ enters your body on the inbreath, and all the way through as the breath      
X+ leaves your body on the outbreath, perhaps noticing the slight pauses between
X+ one inbreath and the following out- breath, and between one outbreath and the
X+ following inbreath.                                                          
X 6. There is no need to try to control the breathing in any way-simply let the 
X+ breath breathe itself. As best you can, also bring this attitude of allowing 
X+ to the rest of your experience. There is nothing to be fixed, no particular  
X+ state to be achieved. As best you can, simply allow your experience to be    
X+ your experience, without needing it to be other than it is.                  
X 7. Sooner or later (usually sooner), your mind will wander away from the focus
X+ on the breath in the lower abdomen to thoughts, planning, daydreams, drifting
X+ along-whatever. This is perfectly OK-it's simply what minds do. It is not a  
X+ mis- take or a failure. When you notice that your awareness is no longer on  
X+ the breath, gently congratulate yourself-you have come back and are once more
X+ aware of your experience! You may want to acknowledge briefly where the mind 
X+ has been ("Ah, there's thinking"). Then, gently escort the awareness back to 
X+ a focus on the changing pattern of physical sensations in the lower abdomen, 
X+ renewing the intention to pay attention to the ongoing inbreath or outbreath,
X+ whichever you find.                                                          
X 8. However often you notice that the mind has wandered (and this will quite   
X+ likely happen over and over and over again), as best you can, congratulate   
X+ yourself each time on reconnecting with your experience in the moment, gently
X+ escorting the attention back to the breath, and simply resume following in   
X+ awareness the changing pattern of physical sensations that come with each    
X+ inbreath and out- breath.                                                    
X 9. As best you can, bring a quality of kindliness to your awareness, perhaps  
X+ see- ing the repeated wanderings of the mind as opportunities to bring       
X+ patience and gentle curiosity to your experience.                            
X 10. Continue with the practice for 10-15 minutes, or longer if you wish,      
X+ perhaps reminding yourself from time to time that the intention is simply to 
X+ be aware of your experience in each moment, as best you can, using the breath
X+ as an anchor to gently reconnect with the here and now each time you notice  
X+ that your mind has wandered and is no longer down in the abdomen, following  
X+ the breath.                                                                  
