Q 20250170_Arbeit.odp                                                           
X 2. Gathering Then redirect your attention to focus on the physical sensations 
X+ of the breathing itself. Move in close to the sense of the breath in the     
X+ abdomen . . . feeling the sensations of the abdomen wall expanding as the    
X+ breath comes in . . . and falling back as the breath goes out. Follow the    
X+ breath all the way in and all the way out, using the breathing to anchor     
X+ yourself into the present. Step                                              
X 3. Expanding Now expand the field of your awareness around the breathing so   
X+ that it includes a sense of the body as a whole, your posture, and facial    
X+ expression. If you become aware of any sensations of discomfort, tension, or 
X+ resistance, take your awareness there by breathing into them on the inbreath.
X+ Then breathe out from those sensations, softening and opening with the       
X+ outbreath. As best you can, bring this expanded awareness to the next moments
X+ of your day.                                                                 
E meditation_27;general;prompt;en                                               
M Mindful Walking                                                               
U Health                                                                        
Q 20250171_Arbeit.odp                                                           
X 1. Find a place where you can walk up and down, without feeling concerned     
X+ about whether people can see you. It can be inside or outside-and the length 
X+ of your "walk" may vary perhaps between 7 and 10 paces.                      
X 2. Stand at one end of your walk, with your feet parallel to each other, about
X+ 4 to 6 inches apart, and your knees "unlocked," so that they can gently flex.
X+ Allow your arms to hang loosely by your sides, or hold your hands loosely    
X+ together in front of your body. Direct your gaze, softly, straight ahead.    
X 3. Bring the focus of your awareness to the bottoms of your feet, getting a   
X+ direct sense of the physical sensations of the contact of the feet with the  
X+ ground and the weight of your body transmitted through your legs and feet to 
X+ the ground. You may find it helpful to flex your knees slightly a few times  
X+ to get a clearer sense of the sensations in the feet and legs.               
X 4. When you are ready, transfer the weight of the body into the right leg,    
X+ noticing the changing pattern of physical sensations in the legs and feet as 
X+ the left leg "emp- ties" and the right leg takes over the support of the rest
X+ of the body.                                                                 
X 5. With the left leg "empty," allow the left heel to rise slowly from the     
X+ floor, noticing the sensations in the calf muscles as you do so, and         
X+ continue, allowing the whole of the left foot to lift gently until only the  
X+ toes are in contact with the floor. Aware of the physical sensations in the  
X+ feet and legs, slowly lift the left foot, carefully move it forward, feeling 
X+ the foot and leg as they move through the air, and place the heel on the     
X+ floor. Allow the rest of the bottom of the left foot to make contact with the
X+ floor as you transfer the weight of the body into the left leg and foot,     
X+ aware of the increasing physical sensations in the left leg and foot, and of 
X+ the "emptying" of the right leg and the right heel leaving the floor.        
X 6. With the weight fully transferred to the left leg, allow the rest of the   
X+ right foot to lift and move it slowly forward, aware of the changing patterns
X+ of physical sensa- tions in the foot and leg as you do so. Focusing your     
X+ attention on the right heel as it makes contact with the ground, transfer the
X+ weight of the body into the right foot as it is placed gently on the ground, 
X+ aware of the shifting pattern of physical sensations in the two legs and     
X+ feet.                                                                        
X 7. In this way, slowly move from one end of your walk to the other, aware     
X+ particularly of the sensations in the bottoms of the feet and heels as they  
X+ make contact with the floor, and of the sensations in the muscles of the legs
X+ as they swing forward.                                                       
X 8. At the end of your walk, stop for a few moments, then turn slowly around,  
X+ aware of and appreciating the complex pattern of movements through which the 
X+ body changes direction, and continue walking.                                
X 9. Walk up and down in this way, being aware, as best you can, of physical    
X+ sensa- tions in the feet and legs, and of the contact of the feet with the   
X+ floor. Keep your gaze directed softly ahead.                                 
X 10. When you notice that the mind has wandered away from awareness of the     
X+ sensa- tions of walking, gently escort the focus of attention back to the    
X+ sensations in the feet and legs, using the sensations as the feet contact the
X+ floor, in particular, as an "anchor" to reconnect with the present moment,   
X+ just as you used the breath in the sitting meditation. If you find your mind 
X+ has wandered, you might find it helpful to stand still for a few moments,    
X+ gathering the focus of attention before resuming your walking.               
X 11. Continue to walk for 10 to 15 minutes, or longer, if you wish.            
X 12. To begin with, walk at a pace that is slower than usual, to give yourself 
X+ a better chance to be fully aware of the sensations of walking. Once you feel
X+ comfortable walking slowly with awareness, you can experiment as well with   
X+ walking at faster speeds, up to and beyond normal walking speed. If you are  
X+ feeling particularly agitated, it may be helpful to begin walking fast, with 
X+ awareness, and to slow down naturally as you settle.                         
X 13. As often as you can, bring the same kind of awareness that you cultivate  
X+ in walk- ing meditation to your normal, everyday experiences of walking.     
