M See Your Thoughts Differently                                                 
U Health                                                                        
Q 20250171_Arbeit.odp                                                           
X 1. Just watch them come in and leave, without feeling that you have to follow 
X+ them.                                                                        
X 2. See if it is possible to notice the feelings that give rise to the         
X+ thoughts: the "context" in which your thoughts are but one link in a chain of
X+ events.                                                                      
X 3. View your thought as a mental event rather than a fact. It may be true that
X+ this event often occurs with other feelings. It is tempting to think of it as
X+ being true, but it is still up to you to decide whether it is true and how   
X+ you want to deal with it.                                                    
X 4. Write your thoughts down on paper. This lets you see them in a way that is 
X+ less emotional and overwhelming. Also, the pause between having the thought  
X+ and writing it down can give you a moment to respond to it differently.      
X 5. For particularly difficult thoughts, it may be helpful to take another look
X+ at them intentionally, in a balanced, open state of mind, as part of your    
X+ sitting practice. Let your "wise mind" give its perspective, perhaps labeling
X+ the feeling out of which, it arises, and holding a sense of curiosity, as    
X+ best you can: "Ah, here is sadness"; "Here is the voice of depression"; "Here
X+ is the familiar harsh and critical voice." The keynote attitude to take with 
X+ your thoughts is gentle interest and curiosity.                              
